Retrieve PROMPT and PLAN, then inspect the current worktree with fresh source, caller, integration, configuration, documentation, test, and command evidence. This review runs when the invocation gate admits it. Delegate independent evidence gathering to concurrent read-only subagents; reconcile findings here. Do not use an implementer summary, rationale, or completion claim as evidence.

### Verification fan-out

Fan out read-only verification across independent criterion groups, one subagent per group. Examples of groups are: **build** (compilation, packaging, install), **lint** (style and lint gates), **types** (static type checks), and **focused tests** (the per-step `Verify` commands, parametrized to a tight scope). These examples are not size prerequisites for delegation. Each subagent returns the four-field shape (`Expected`, `Observed`, `Evidence`, `Location`) defined in the shared subagent guidance above.

Every subagent lead a verdict depends on — passing or failing — is a lead, never evidence by itself: before writing the verdict, reproduce the lead in the main session (re-run the command, or re-read the cited `path:line`) and cite the reproduced result. A lead you did not reproduce is not a verdict; reconcile the reproduced observation with the subagent's report and keep the disagreement if the conflict is real. Reconcile all group findings here in the main session before any verdict is written.

Treat the unchanged request and its acceptance criteria as authoritative. A plan step is an implementation route, not a new product requirement unless its outcome is an explicit acceptance criterion. Independently classify each required plan reference as completed, adapted, not applicable, or blocked:

- `adapted`: fresh evidence required a different route and the intended outcome still holds.
- `not_applicable`: the plan assumption conflicts with or is already satisfied by the worktree, while every affected request criterion remains covered.
- `blocked`: necessary work remains and cannot currently be completed.

Do not reject a correct implementation only because an inaccurate plan step changed. Derive each disposition from PROMPT, PLAN, and fresh workspace evidence.

Choose the outcome from fresh evidence, not the developer's status:

- `completed`: every unchanged request criterion is met, every required plan
  item has been individually proven, and no necessary plan work remains.
- `request_changes`: localized unmet work is actionable in this development cycle. The body describes what fell short, what evidence supports it, and what the next agent should do.
- `failed`: a criterion is impossible, contradictory, or not evaluable, or necessary work has no safe actionable route.

`failed` records stronger or not-evaluable evidence for explicit resolution; the label itself never fails the pipeline.

## Free-form decision body

The validator only checks the frontmatter ``status`` enum. The body is the next agent's reading matter; write it in your own words. A useful pattern is to describe what fell short, the evidence that supports the judgement, and (for ``request_changes``) the leftover work the next agent should pick up. ``request_changes`` is the only safe status when localized unmet work is actionable in this development cycle — split the remaining work into independent units and dispatch them in parallel rather than handing each one back as a separate ``partial`` decision. A whole-change review of the implementation belongs here, too: the parallel pieces fit together, nothing outside the plan regressed, no unrelated scope, and the change follows repository policy.

{% set development_decision_example %}
---
type: development_analysis_decision
status: request_changes
---

The focused regression test for oversized indexes is missing; the rest of
the work is sound. `pytest tests/test_foo.py -q` reports 18 passed but
contains no oversized-index case. A developer cycle should add the
missing case and re-run the focused tests.

The remaining work splits into one independent unit that a worker can
pick up: WU-X owns `tests/test_foo.py`; add a parametrized
oversized-index case there; the unit check is
`pytest tests/test_foo.py -q -k oversized` exits 0 with at least one
new oversized case. Dispatch WU-X in parallel with the rest of the
cycle rather than handing each unit back as a separate `partial`.
{% endset %}

